  "problem": "${(16{j}^{2}{k}^{6})}^{1/2}=$",
  "worked_solution": "We apply the power of product rule and then simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(16{j}^{2}{k}^{6})}^{1/2} & = \\\\ {({4}^{2}{j}^{2}{k}^{6})}^{1/2} & = \\\\ {({4}^{2})}^{1/2}\\cdot {({j}^{2})}^{1/2}\\cdot {({k}^{6})}^{1/2} & = \\\\ {4}^{2\\cdot (1/2)}\\cdot {j}^{2\\cdot (1/2)}\\cdot {k}^{6\\cdot (1/2)} & = \\\\ {4}^{2/2}\\cdot {j}^{2/2}\\cdot {k}^{6/2} & = \\\\ 4j{k}^{3} & \\end{aligned}\n$$",
